For several years, Carl Jung began each morning by sketching a small circular
drawing in a notebook. Only later did he recognize what he had been doing: each
circle was a kind of self-portrait, a daily reading of his inner state.

## The form that drew itself

Jung found that his patients, during periods of psychological crisis or
reorganization, spontaneously produced circular, centered drawings, without
being asked and usually without knowing the religious traditions they echoed.

## A portrait of the Self

He came to call the mandala "the psychological expression of the totality of
the self," a symbol of wholeness that the psyche reaches for when it is trying
to reorder itself around a center.

